# .trivyignore — CVEs the pipeline tolerates because no upstream fix
# is available yet. Each entry carries a reason and a review date so
# this file does not become a dumping ground for silenced findings.
#
# Policy (per ADR-0032):
#   - Only CVEs with no patched version upstream may be added here.
#   - Every entry MUST have a comment with the reason + a review date.
#   - On every CI run, Trivy still prints these to the log — the ignore
#     only suppresses the exit-code block, not the visibility.
#   - Review this file monthly and remove entries whose upstream fixes
#     have landed. If an entry is older than 90 days with no upstream
#     progress, open a GitHub issue to track the gap.
#
# Last reviewed: 2026-10-10

# ─── Fastify ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No upstream patch as of 2026-10-10. The fastify maintainers have
# acknowledged these in their security advisories but no release has
# shipped the fix. Monitor:
#   https://github.com/fastify/fastify/security/advisories
# Re-review: 2026-11-10
CVE-2026-84428
CVE-2026-84469
CVE-2026-84504

# ─── OpenSSL in distroless base ─────────────────────────────────────
# These CVEs have no upstream fix as of 2026-10-10. The Dockerfile
# base is pinned to the current latest distroless digest, so any
# openssl patch released upstream will arrive here on the next base
# digest rotation (see Dockerfile for the rotation query).
#
# Note: the CVE IDs in this file must track Debian's security DB,
# which periodically reorganises CVE assignments. First-ever CI run
# surfaced CVE-2026-28389/28390 for the same openssl package; those
# IDs were reorganised to -28387/-28388 within hours. Lesson learned:
# .trivyignore entries can go stale not just because a fix lands but
# because the vuln DB restructures. Re-verify IDs on every monthly
# review.
# Re-review: 2026-11-10
CVE-2026-28387
CVE-2026-28388
